#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d99805e-a17a-57ef-ae56-85f3060b066a" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations From Logarithmic Form to Exponential Form"}] :question/id #uuid "0cd0e716-e9da-51ca-9c75-adedad1bf12d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3978" :question/problem "The equation $\\log_3{9} = 2$ is equivalent to $b^2 = 9$. What is the value of $b$?" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_3{9} = 2\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get $a = 9$, $b = 3$, and $x = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $3^2 = 9$. Consequently, $b = 3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2812181f-7ccc-5609-a399-1cf68591a903" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations From Logarithmic Form to Exponential Form with Exponents of Zero or One"}] :question/id #uuid "c9eafa03-bc10-5dfd-a212-a662d26974ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3979" :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{6}{1} = 0$ is equivalent to $b^0 = 1$. What is the value of $b$?" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_{6}{1} = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get $a = 1$, $b = 6$, and $x = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $6^0 = 1$. Consequently, $b = 6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aedb775e-d1c1-54dd-b66a-a9351dff1d97" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations From Logarithmic Form to Exponential Form Using Fractional Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "446ff062-5a2d-5178-9660-60bafae375fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3980" :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{16}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b} = 4$. What is the value of $b$?" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_{16}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get $a = 4$, $b = 16$, and\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n16^{1/2} = 4\n$$\n\nwhich is also equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16} = 4\n$$\n\nConsequently, $b = 16$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c754b280-89d0-5a8b-a1bc-a1be3d3ffce6" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations From Logarithmic Form to Exponential Form Using Negative Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "2613759b-9a0e-59b1-952b-1d2a8a08b4f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3981" :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8} \\right) = -3$ is equivalent to $b^{-3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}$. What is the value of $b$?" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8} \\right) = -3\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\n$b = 2$, and $x = -3$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2^{-3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $b = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aedb775e-d1c1-54dd-b66a-a9351dff1d97" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations From Logarithmic Form to Exponential Form Using Fractional Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "439b77bc-4301-5739-aa18-4a33b0088491" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} 